[b]Automatic window tiling for KDE Plasma 6[/b]

Open or close an app and ktiler adjusts the layout. Use keyboard shortcuts to focus, swap, or move windows between monitors without dragging them into place.

[list]
[*] [b]Choose a layout for each monitor.[/b] Use binary splits, columns, or rows.
[*] [b]Drag a shared edge to give a window more room.[/b] In rows and columns, Spread Resize shares the adjustment across neighboring windows.
[*] [b]Pause tiling when you need to.[/b] Press Alt+F3 and open the ktiler submenu.
[/list]

[b]Default keyboard shortcuts[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Alt+W/A/S/D[/b] — Focus a window up, left, down, or right. At an edge, focus can cross to the next monitor.
[*] [b]Alt+Shift+W/A/S/D[/b] — Swap with a tile in that direction or move the window to the next monitor.
[*] [b]Alt+Shift+1 / 2 / 3[/b] — Switch to binary splits, columns, or rows, respectively.
[*] [b]Alt+Shift+R / Alt+Shift+E[/b] — Rotate clockwise or counterclockwise.
[*] [b]Alt+Shift+K[/b] — Pause or resume tiling.
[/list]

The layout shortcuts above use the top number row on a US keyboard. KDE shows them as Alt+!, Alt+@, and Alt+#. You can change any binding in [b]Configure ktiler → Shortcuts[/b].

[b]After installing[/b]
Open [b]System Settings → KWin Scripts[/b], tick [b]ktiler[/b], and click [b]Apply[/b]. Use the [b]Configure[/b] button next to ktiler to change its settings.

[b]Compatibility[/b]
Requires [b]KDE Plasma 6.3 or newer in a Wayland session[/b]. Kubuntu 26.04 LTS meets both requirements out of the box. Plasma 5 and X11 sessions are not supported.

